=\frac{24/47}{4}
=\frac{6}{47}\text{ A}
\tag{i}\] <img src="img/902817b952c07b4a.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,224px)"> Applying KCL at node \(B\), \[\frac{V_B}{3}+\frac{V_B}{5}+\frac{V_B-2}{4}=0\] \[20V_B+12V_B+15V_B-30=0\] \[V_B=\frac{30}{47}\text{ V}\] From Fig.~(b), \[I_2=\frac{V_B}{5}
=\frac{30/47}{5}
=\frac{6}{47}\text{ A}
\tag{ii}\] From equations (i) and (ii), \[\left|\frac{I_1}{I_2}\right|
=
\frac{6/47}{6/47}
=1\] <div class="sol-block sol-method"><div class="sol-block-title">Method Reciprocity Theorem</div></div> According to reciprocity theorem, in a linear network, ratio of response to excitation remains constant even if the position of source and response are interchanged. In the given network, the position of source and response are interchanged. Therefore, \[\frac{I_1}{2}
=
\frac{I_2}{2}\] \[I_1=I_2\] Hence, \[\left|\frac{I_1}{I_2}\right|=1\] \(1\) <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ol><li>Reciprocity theorem is applicable only for linear and reciprocal passive networks.</li><li>Reciprocity theorem is valid only when the network has a single independent source.</li><li>For multiple independent sources, reciprocity theorem is applied along with superposition theorem.</li></ol></div>

Given: \[x(t)=2\cos\!\left(\frac{2\pi}{3}t\right)+\cos(\pi t)\] <div class="sol-block sol-method"><div class="sol-block-title">Method Method 1</div>\[\omega_1=\frac{2\pi}{3}\ \text{rad/sec},
\qquad
T_1=\frac{2\pi}{\omega_1}
=\frac{2\pi}{2\pi/3}
=3~\text{sec}\] \[\omega_2=\pi\ \text{rad/sec},
\qquad
T_2=\frac{2\pi}{\omega_2}
=\frac{2\pi}{\pi}
=2~\text{sec}\] Fundamental time period of \(T_1\) and \(T_2\) is calculated as, <div class="table-scroll"><table class="qtable"><tr><td></td><td>\(\times 1\)</td><td>\(\times 2\)</td><td>\(\times 3\)</td></tr><tr><td>\(T_1=3\)</td><td>3</td><td>6</td><td>9</td></tr><tr><td>\(T_2=2\)</td><td>2</td><td>4</td><td>6</td></tr></table></div> Fundamental time period, \[T_0=6~\text{sec}\] Hence, fundamental time period of the signal is \(6~\text{sec}\).</div> <div class="sol-block sol-method"><div class="sol-block-title">Method Method 2</div>\[\omega_1=\frac{2\pi}{3}\ \text{rad/sec}\] \[\omega_2=\pi\ \text{rad/sec}\] Fundamental angular frequency is given by, \[\omega_0=\mathrm{HCF}(\omega_1,\omega_2)\] \[\omega_0
=
\mathrm{HCF}\!\left(\frac{2\pi}{3},\pi\right)
=
\frac{\pi}{3}\] \[T_0=\frac{2\pi}{\omega_0}
=
\frac{2\pi}{\pi/3}
=
6\] Hence, fundamental time period of the signal is \(6~\text{sec}\).</div> 6

Since the diode is ideal, the circuit acts as a half-wave rectifier. The output voltage across the load is \[v_o(t)=
\begin{cases}
10\sin(100\pi t), &amp; \sin(100\pi t)\ge0,\\
0, &amp; \sin(100\pi t)&lt;0.
\end{cases}\] The RMS value of a <strong>half-wave rectified sine wave</strong> is \[V_{\mathrm{rms}}
=
\frac{V_m}{2}
=
\frac{10}{2}
=
5~\text{V}.\] Hence, the average power dissipated in the load resistance is \[P_{\mathrm{avg}}
=
\frac{V_{\mathrm{rms}}^2}{R_L}
=
\frac{5^2}{100}
=
0.25~\text{W}.\] Hence, \[\boxed{0.25~\text{W}}\]

Since the two NMOS transistors are identical and are operating in saturation, both will have same saturation drain current, \[\frac{k}{2}(V_{GS1}-V_T)^2
=
\frac{k}{2}(V_{GS2}-V_T)^2\] \[\Rightarrow V_{GS1}=V_{GS2}\] For the upper NMOS, \[V_{GS1}
=
30-V_{\text{out}}\] For the lower NMOS, \[V_{GS2}
=
10-0
=
10~\text{V}\] Equating the two gate-to-source voltages, \[30-V_{\text{out}}
=
10\] \[V_{\text{out}}
=
20~\text{V}\] Therefore, \[\boxed{20}\]

The feedback network is a non-inverting amplifier with \[\beta=\frac{1\,\text{k}\Omega}{9\,\text{k}\Omega+1\,\text{k}\Omega}=0.1.\] The open-loop gain of the op-amp is \[A_v(s)=\frac{10^4}{1+10^{-3}s}.\] The closed-loop transfer function is \[\frac{V_o}{V_{in}}
=\frac{A_v(s)}{1+\beta A_v(s)}
=
\frac{\dfrac{10^4}{1+10^{-3}s}}
{1+\dfrac{10^3}{1+10^{-3}s}}.\] Simplifying, \[\frac{V_o}{V_{in}}
=
\frac{10^4}{1001+10^{-3}s}
=
\frac{\dfrac{10^4}{1001}}
{1+\dfrac{10^{-3}}{1001}s}.\] Comparing with the standard first-order form, \(\frac{K}{1+\dfrac{s}{\omega_B}},\) gives \[\omega_B=\frac{1001}{10^{-3}}
=1001\times10^3
=1.001\times10^6\,\text{rad/s}.\] Therefore, the \(3\)-dB bandwidth is \[\boxed{\text{(d) } 10^6}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>Feedback factor: \(\beta=\dfrac{R_1}{R_1+R_f}\).</li><li>Closed-loop transfer function: \(\dfrac{A}{1+\beta A}\).</li><li>Negative feedback increases the bandwidth by the factor \(1+\beta A_0\).</li></ul></div>

The given transfer function is \[G(s)=\frac{s^{2}-bs+c}{s^{2}+bs+c}.\] Substitute \(s=j\omega\): \[G(j\omega)=\frac{(c-\omega^{2})-jb\omega}{(c-\omega^{2})+jb\omega}.\] Hence, \[|G(j\omega)|
=\frac{\sqrt{(c-\omega^{2})^{2}+(b\omega)^{2}}}
{\sqrt{(c-\omega^{2})^{2}+(b\omega)^{2}}}
=1.\] Since the magnitude remains unity for all frequencies and only the phase changes with frequency, the network is an <strong>all-pass filter</strong>. \[\boxed{\text{(c) an all-pass filter}}\]

The controllability matrix is \[\mathcal{C}=
\begin{bmatrix}
B &amp; AB
\end{bmatrix}\] First, compute \[AB=
\begin{bmatrix}
1 &amp; 2\\
\alpha &amp; 6
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
1\\
1
\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
3\\
\alpha+6
\end{bmatrix}\] Hence, \[\mathcal{C}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
1 &amp; 3\\
1 &amp; \alpha+6
\end{bmatrix}.\] For the system to be uncontrollable, \(|\mathcal{C}|=0\) \[\begin{vmatrix}
1 &amp; 3\\
1 &amp; \alpha+6
\end{vmatrix}
=0\] \[\alpha+6-3=0\] \[\alpha+3=0\] \[\boxed{\alpha=-3}\]

Given \(\log_{x}(5/7) = -1/3\). By definition of logarithm: \[x^{-1/3} = \frac{5}{7}
    \implies x^{1/3} = \frac{7}{5}
    \implies x = \left(\frac{7}{5}\right)^{3}
    = \frac{343}{125}\] \[\boxed{\text{A) }\dfrac{343}{125}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>\(\log_{x} a = b \Leftrightarrow x^{b} = a\).</li><li>Negative exponent: \(x^{-1/3} = 5/7
            \Rightarrow x^{1/3} = 7/5\).</li></ul></div>

A \(3\times3\times3\) cube made of \(27\) unit cubes (\(1\times1\times1\) each). <strong>Visible faces:</strong> Only the outer surface faces are visible. Total outer surface area \(= 6 \times 3^{2}
  = 54\) unit faces. <strong>Not visible faces:</strong> Total faces of all 27 unit cubes \(= 27 \times 6 = 162\). Faces not visible \(= 162 - 54 = 108\). <strong>Ratio</strong> (visible : not visible): \[54 : 108 = 1 : 2\] \[\boxed{\text{C) }1:2}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>Visible faces \(= 6s^{2}\) for an \(s \times s
            \times s\) cube.</li><li>Total faces \(= s^{3} \times 6\); not visible \(=\) total \(-\) visible.</li></ul></div>

Electricity consumption per km for each stretch: \[\begin{array}{ll}
      \text{M:} &amp; \dfrac{12}{20}
                  = 0.60\,\text{kWh/km} \\ \text{N:} &amp; \dfrac{13}{25}
                  = 0.52\,\text{kWh/km}
    \end{array}\] \[\begin{array}{ll}
      \text{O:} &amp; \dfrac{20}{30}
                  = 0.67\,\text{kWh/km} \\ \text{P:} &amp; \dfrac{12}{25}
                  = 0.48\,\text{kWh/km}
    \end{array}\] Minimum electricity per km is at stretch <strong>P</strong> (\(0.48\,\text{kWh/km}\)). \[\boxed{\text{D) P}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>Electricity per km \(=\) incremental electricity \(\div\) incremental distance for each stretch.</li><li>Use cumulative values from the table: subtract the previous row to get the stretch values.</li></ul></div>

\(P(\text{Ram}) = \tfrac{1}{6}\), \(P(\text{Ramesh}) = \tfrac{1}{8}\). Probability that <strong>only one</strong> is selected: \[P = P(R)\cdot(1-P(Re)) + (1-P(R))\cdot P(Re)\] \[= \frac{1}{6}\cdot\frac{7}{8}
      + \frac{5}{6}\cdot\frac{1}{8}
    = \frac{7}{48} + \frac{5}{48}
    = \frac{12}{48}
    = \frac{1}{4}\] \[\boxed{\text{B) }\dfrac{1}{4}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>\(P(\text{only one}) = P(A)\cdot P(\bar{B})
            + P(\bar{A})\cdot P(B)\).</li><li>Events are independent — multiply individual probabilities.</li></ul></div>

A tiger is 50 leaps behind a deer. Tiger: 5 leaps/min, 8,m/leap. Deer: 4 leaps/min, 5,m/leap. <strong>Speed of tiger:</strong><br/>\[v_T = 5 \times 8 = 40\,\text{m/min}\] <strong>Speed of deer:</strong><br/>\[v_D = 4 \times 5 = 20\,\text{m/min}\] <strong>Initial gap:</strong> \(50\) tiger leaps \(= 50 \times 8
  = 400\,\text{m}\). <strong>Relative speed:</strong><br/>\[v_{\text{rel}} = 40 - 20 = 20\,\text{m/min}\] <strong>Time to catch:</strong><br/>\[t = \frac{400}{20} = 20\,\text{min}\] <strong>Distance tiger runs:</strong><br/>\[d = 40 \times 20 = 800\,\text{m}\] \[\boxed{800}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>Speed \(=\) leaps/min \(\times\) metres/leap.</li><li>Initial gap in metres \(=\) leaps \(\times\) length of tiger's leap.</li><li>Time \(= \dfrac{\text{gap}}
            {v_{\text{tiger}} - v_{\text{deer}}}\).</li></ul></div>

Circular sheet of radius \(R = 30\,\text{cm}\). A sector of \(10\%\) area is removed, leaving \(90\%\) of the sheet. <strong>Remaining arc length</strong> (becomes the base circumference of the cone): \[\text{Arc} = 0.9 \times 2\pi R = 0.9 \times 60\pi
    = 54\pi\] Let \(r\) = base radius of cone, \(l\) = slant height. Slant height \(= R = 30\,\text{cm}\). \[2\pi r = 54\pi \implies r = 27\,\text{cm}\] <strong>Height of cone:</strong><br/>\[h = \sqrt{l^{2} - r^{2}} = \sqrt{900 - 729}
    = \sqrt{171} = 3\sqrt{19}\,\text{cm}\] <strong>Ratio of radius to height:</strong><br/>\[\frac{r}{h} = \frac{27}{3\sqrt{19}}
    = \frac{9}{\sqrt{19}}\] \[\boxed{\text{1.90 to 2.20}}\]

\[S = \log\tan1^{\circ} + \log\tan2^{\circ}
      + \cdots + \log\tan89^{\circ}\] \[= \sum_{k=1}^{89} \log\tan k^{\circ}\] Note: \(\tan(90^{\circ}-k^{\circ}) = \cot k^{\circ}
  = \dfrac{1}{\tan k^{\circ}}\), so: \[\log\tan k^{\circ} + \log\tan(90^{\circ}-k^{\circ})
    = \log\tan k^{\circ} + \log\cot k^{\circ}
    = \log 1 = 0\] Pairing \(k\) with \(90-k\) for \(k=1\) to \(44\) gives 44 zero pairs, and the middle term is \(\log\tan45^{\circ}
  = \log 1 = 0\). \[S = 0\] \[\boxed{\text{C) }0}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>\(\tan k^{\circ} \cdot \tan(90^{\circ}-k^{\circ})
            = 1 \Rightarrow \log\tan k^{\circ}
            + \log\tan(90^{\circ}-k^{\circ}) = 0\).</li><li>\(\log\tan 45^{\circ} = \log 1 = 0\).</li></ul></div>

Given: \(\angle Q\) is a right angle, \(PS:QS = 3:1\), \(RT:QT = 5:2\), \(PU:UR = 1:1\). Area of \(\triangle QTS = 20\,\text{cm}^{2}\). <img src="img/a90f2cb43e06938f.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,205px)"> <strong>Step 1 — Area of \(\triangle QTR\):</strong> \(RT:QT = 5:2\), so \(\triangle QTR\) and \(\triangle QTS\) share the same base \(QT\). Using the ratio of heights from \(R\) and \(S\): \[\frac{\text{Area}(\triangle QTR)}
         {\text{Area}(\triangle QTS)}
    = \frac{RT}{QT} = \frac{5}{2}\] \[\text{Area}(\triangle QTR)
    = \frac{5}{2} \times 20 = 50\,\text{cm}^{2}\] <strong>Step 2 — Area of \(\triangle QSR\):</strong><br/>\[\text{Area}(\triangle QSR)
    = \text{Area}(\triangle QTS)
    + \text{Area}(\triangle QTR)
    = 20 + 50 = 70\,\text{cm}^{2}\] <strong>Step 3 — Area of \(\triangle PQR\):</strong> \(PS:QS = 3:1\), so \(PQ = PS + SQ = 3+1 = 4\) parts, \(QS = 1\) part. \(\triangle PQR\) and \(\triangle QSR\) share the same height from \(R\). Their bases are \(PQ\) and \(QS\): \[\frac{\text{Area}(\triangle PQR)}
         {\text{Area}(\triangle QSR)}
    = \frac{PQ}{QS} = \frac{4}{1}\] \[\text{Area}(\triangle PQR)
    = 4 \times 70 = 280\,\text{cm}^{2}\] \[\boxed{280.00}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>Triangles sharing the <strong>same height</strong> have areas in the ratio of their bases.</li><li>Build up the area step by step: start from the known small triangle and scale using the given ratios.</li><li>\(PS:QS = 3:1 \Rightarrow PQ:QS = 4:1\), giving the final scaling factor of 4.</li></ul></div> <div class="sol-block sol-mistakes"><div class="sol-block-title">Mistakes to avoid</div><ul><li>Do <strong>not</strong> confuse \(PS:QS = 3:1\) with \(PQ:QS\) — always add the ratio parts to get the full base \(PQ = 4\) units.</li><li>The ratio \(PU:UR = 1:1\) is used to confirm \(U\) is the midpoint of \(PR\), but is not needed for the area calculation here.</li></ul></div>

A coin is tossed three times. <ul><li>\(X\): head in each of first two tosses.</li><li>\(Y\): tail on third toss.</li><li>\(Z\): two tails in three tosses.</li></ul> \[P(X) = \frac{1}{4},\quad P(Y) = \frac{1}{2},\quad
    P(Z) = \binom{3}{2}\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{3}
    = \frac{3}{8}\] \[P(X \cap Y) = P(\text{HHT}) = \frac{1}{8}
    = P(X) \cdot P(Y) \implies X,Y \textbf{ independent.}\] \[P(Y \cap Z):\ \text{events with tail on toss 3 and
    exactly 2 tails} = P(\text{HTT or THT}) = \frac{2}{8}\] \[P(Y)\cdot P(Z) = \frac{1}{2}\cdot\frac{3}{8}
    = \frac{3}{16} \neq \frac{2}{8} = \frac{1}{4}
    \implies Y,Z \textbf{ dependent.}\] \[\boxed{\text{B) Y and Z are dependent}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>Independence: \(P(A \cap B) = P(A)\cdot P(B)\).</li><li>Compute \(P(A \cap B)\) by listing favorable outcomes explicitly.</li></ul></div>

\(A = \{2,3,4,5\}\), \(B = \{11,12,13,14,15\}\). One number is chosen from each set. Pairs that sum to 16: \[(2,14),\;(3,13),\;(4,12),\;(5,11)
    \implies 4\text{ favourable pairs}\] Total pairs \(= 4 \times 5 = 20\). \[P = \frac{4}{20} = 0.20\] \[\boxed{\text{A) }0.20}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>List all pairs \((a,b)\) with \(a+b=16\) systematically.</li><li>\(P = \dfrac{\text{favourable pairs}}
            {\text{total pairs}}\).</li></ul></div>

Functions defined for distinct integers \(p, q, r, s\): \[f(p,q,r,s) = \max(p,q,r,s), \qquad
    g(p,q,r,s) = \min(p,q,r,s)\] \[h(p,q,r,s) =
    \begin{cases}
      \text{remainder of } \dfrac{p \times q}{r \times s}
      &amp; \text{if } p \times q &gt; r \times s \\ \text{remainder of } \dfrac{r \times s}{p \times q}
      &amp; \text{if } r \times s &gt; p \times q
    \end{cases}\] \[fgh(p,q,r,s) = f(p,q,r,s) \times g(p,q,r,s)
                  \times h(p,q,r,s)\] Evaluate \(fg(h(2,5,7,3),\ 4,\ 6,\ 8)\): <strong>Step 1 — Evaluate \(h(2,5,7,3)\):</strong><br/>\[p \times q = 2 \times 5 = 10, \qquad
    r \times s = 7 \times 3 = 21\] Since \(r \times s = 21 &gt; p \times q = 10\): \[h(2,5,7,3) = \text{remainder of } \frac{21}{10} = 1\] <strong>Step 2 — Evaluate \(f(p,q)\) with two variables:</strong> Now compute \(fg(h, 4, 6, 8)\) where \(h = 1\), giving the set \(\{1, 4, 6, 8\}\): \[f(1,4,6,8) = \max(1,4,6,8) = 8\] \[g(1,4,6,8) = \min(1,4,6,8) = 1\] <strong>Step 3 — Evaluate \(fg\):</strong> The function \(fg(p,q,r,s) = f \times g\): \[fg(1,4,6,8) = 8 \times 1 = 8\] \[\boxed{8.00}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>Always evaluate the <strong>innermost</strong> function first: compute \(h(2,5,7,3)\) before using its result as an argument.</li><li>\(h\) computes the remainder of the <strong>larger</strong> product divided by the <strong>smaller</strong> product.</li><li>\(fg = f \times g = \max \times \min\).</li></ul></div>

Set up coordinates. M is north of N at 4,km: \(N = (0,0)\), \(M = (0,4)\). P is south of O at 2,km and southeast of O by 1,km. N is southeast of O \(\Rightarrow\) O is northwest of N. \[O = N + \left(-\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}},
    \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\right)
    \approx (-0.707,\,0.707)\] P is south of O by 2,km: \[P = O + (0,-2) \approx (-0.707,\,-1.293)\] Distance MP: \[d = \sqrt{(0-(-0.707))^{2}+(4-(-1.293))^{2}}
      = \sqrt{0.5 + 28.02}
      \approx \sqrt{28.52}
      \approx 5.34\,\text{km}\] \[\boxed{\text{A) }5.34\,\text{km}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>Set up Cartesian coordinates with one known point at the origin; resolve directional distances into \(x\) and \(y\) components.</li><li>Southeast \(= 45^{\circ}\) below east: \((\cos(-45^{\circ}), \sin(-45^{\circ}))
            = (1/\sqrt{2}, -1/\sqrt{2})\).</li></ul></div>
